.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt NOVI-INSTALL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm novi-install
.Nd install Novi Linux onto a disk
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm install Fl -disk Ar device Op Ar option ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs on the live system and writes Novi to a disk.
.Cm list
shows candidate targets;
.Cm install
partitions, formats, copies and makes the disk bootable.
.Pp
Firmware is detected from
.Pa /sys/firmware/efi ,
which is the kernel's own record of how this machine got here, and
the two paths differ:
.Bl -column -offset indent "UEFI" "GPT via novi-gpt" "self-contained BOOTX64.EFI"
.It Sy "" Ta Sy table Ta Sy bootloader
.It UEFI Ta GPT Ta Pa /EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI No on the ESP
.It BIOS Ta MBR Ta Pa boot.img No + Pa core.img No in the post-MBR gap
.El
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width "--hostname name"
.It Fl -disk Ar device
The target, for example
.Pa /dev/sda .
Required.
.It Fl -hostname Ar name
Hostname for the installed system.
The default keeps the current one.
It is set with
.Xr novi-state 1 ,
never by editing the file, so the document keeps its comments.
.It Fl -firmware Cm uefi | bios
Default: whatever this machine booted with, which is almost always
what you want.
Installing the other one produces a disk this machine cannot boot.
.It Fl -esp-mib Ar n
Size of the EFI System partition.
Default 512.
.It Fl -encrypt
LUKS2-encrypt the root filesystem, prompting for a passphrase.
The kernel, the initramfs and the boot menu stay
.Sy unencrypted :
something has to be readable before the initramfs can ask.
This protects data at rest against someone who takes the disk.
It is
.Em not
protection against someone who can write to the disk and hand it
back.
.It Fl -ab
Lay the disk out for atomic updates: a shared
.Pa /boot ,
two root slots, and a shared state partition holding
.Pa /home ,
.Pa /var/log ,
.Pa /var/lib/novi-state ,
.Pa /var/lib/alsa
and
.Pa /var/cache/pkg .
The machine boots from slot A.
Slot B is populated by
.Xr novi-slot 8 .
.Pp
It exists at install time because a partition table is written once:
a layout without the second slot could never gain one without a
reinstall.
Refused with
.Fl -encrypt ,
because carving three volumes out of one LUKS container is what LVM
is for and this system has none; leaving
.Pa /home
in the clear for somebody who typed
.Fl -encrypt
would be a silent, serious regression.
.It Fl -slot-mib Ar n
Size of each root slot with
.Fl -ab .
Default 4096.
Measured: the base OS is about 780 MB, so at 2048 a slot is 43% full
with nothing installed.
.It Fl -profile Cm desktop | console
.Cm desktop
is the default.
It installs the desktop packages from the repository on this medium
and declares them, so the installed machine boots into a graphical
session with no network involved.
.It Fl -mirror Ar url
Package mirror for the installed system.
Without it the shipped
.Pa pkg.conf
line is commented out, because the default points at the
installation medium and every installed machine would otherwise get a
.Nm pkg sync
that fails against a directory nobody chose.
.It Fl -user Ar name
Create an account.
It starts locked;
passwd(1)
unlocks it.
A password hash never goes into
.Xr system.conf 5 .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/run/live/novi-repo" -compact
.It Pa /run/live/novi-repo
The signed package repository on the installation medium.
.It Pa /novi-boot
The bootloader pieces generated on the build host.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# novi-install list
# novi-install install --disk /dev/vda --hostname workshop
.Ed
.Pp
An encrypted console machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# novi-install install --disk /dev/sda --encrypt --profile console
.Ed
.Pp
A machine that can be updated atomically later:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# novi-install install --disk /dev/sda --ab --slot-mib 4096
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr novi-state 1 ,
.Xr pkg 1 ,
.Xr system.conf 5 ,
.Xr novi-slot 8
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0003, RFC 0008, RFC 0018, RFC 0041.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Pa bootx64.efi
is signed by a key this build generated, which no stock machine
trusts.
The certificate ships at
.Pa /novi-boot/novi-secureboot.der
for somebody who enrolls their own keys.
This is not Secure Boot support.
